Unfortunately, JSON is a data interchange format and does not have built-in sorting capabilities. However, in JavaScript, you can convert a JSON object into an array of key-value pairs (known as an "object spread") and then use the Array.prototype.sort() method with a custom comparator function to sort based on the desired keys. Here's a function that implements this:

```javascript
const sortJsonByKeys = (jsonObject, keys) => {
  const sortedKeys = [...keys]; // Make a copy so we don't modify the original
  sortedKeys.reverse(); // Sort them in descending order for stability

  return Object.entries(jsonObject).sort((a, b) => {
    for (let I = 0; I < sortedKeys.length; i++) {
      const leftVal = a[sortedKeys[i]] ?? ""; // Get the value for the current key or default to an empty string
      const rightVal = b[sortedKeys[i]] ?? "";
      if (leftVal !== rightVal) { // If the values differ, compare them
        return leftVal > rightVal ? 1 : -1; // Ascending order
        // return leftVal < rightVal ? 1 : -1; // Descending order
      }
    }
    return 0; // Values are equal
  }).reduce((acc, [k, v]) => ({ ...acc, [k]: v }), {}); // Convert back to an object
}

// Example usage
const json = { a: 3, b: 1, c: 2 };
const keys = ["c", "b"];
const sortedJson = sortJsonByKeys(json, keys);
console.log(sortedJson);
```

This function takes two arguments: `jsonObject`, which is the JSON object you want to sort, and `keys`, which is an array of strings representing the keys you want to sort by. It returns a new object with the same structure as the input, but with the keys sorted according to the specified order. The `sortJsonByKeys()` function works by first making a copy of the `keys` array and reversing its order, which ensures stable sorting (i.e., objects with equal values for all comparison keys will be sorted in the same order). Then

