Here's a simple C function that converts the arguments passed in `argv` (an array of null-terminated strings) into a single null-terminated string:

```c
char *join_args(int argc, char **argv) {
    size_t len = 0;
    for (int I = 0; I < argc; i++) {
        len += strlen(argv[i]) + 1; // Add space between arguments
    }

    char *result = malloc(len);
    for (int I = 0, j = 0; I < argc; i++) {
        result[j++] = *(argv[i]); // Copy first character
        while (*(++argv[i])) { // Copy remaining characters, skipping spaces
            result[j++] = *(argv[i]);
        }
        if (i != argc - 1) { // Add space between arguments, except last one
            result[j++] = ' ';
        }
    }
    result[j] = '\0';
    return result;
}
```

This function keeps track of the total length of the resulting string and dynamically allocates memory for it. It then iterates over each argument in `argv`, copies its first character, and skips any spaces before copying the rest of the characters. Finally, it adds a space between arguments (except for the last one), and terminates the string with a null byte.

Note that this function assumes there is at most one space between arguments, which is true for standard command line parsing conventions. If you allow multiple spaces or other separators between arguments, you may need to modify this function accordingly. Also note that the function returns a dynamically allocated string, so you should remember to free it when you're done using it.

