check_numeric <- function(x) {
  x[is.na(x)] <- 0   # replace NA values with 0
  tryCatch(as.numeric(x), error = function(e) NULL)
}

# Example usage:
my_vector <- c("1", "2", "3", "four", "5", NA, "six")
if (all(!is.na(check_numeric(my_vector)))) {
  print("All non-NA elements can be converted to numeric.")
} else {
  print("Not all non-NA elements can be converted to numeric.")
}
```

This function `check_numeric()` takes a character vector as input and first replaces any NA values with 0 using the `[<-` operator. This is done because `as.numeric()` cannot handle NA values and will throw an error. Then it uses `tryCatch()` to catch any errors that may occur when converting the vector to numeric. If there are no errors, the converted vector is returned. If there is an error, `NULL` is returned instead. The `all()` and `!is.na()` functions are then used to check whether all non-NA elements could be successfully converted to numeric. Note that this function assumes that non-numeric strings will be replaced with 0. If you want different behavior for non-numeric strings, you can modify the code inside the `tryCatch()` block.

